"91_winui_shell.mst — WG1, the window and the materials
 (docs/win_gui_design.md §2.1/§2.2, docs/sprints/sprint_wg1_detail.md).
 Loaded via world/winui.list, AFTER 90_winui_probe.mst, which forward-
 declares the two classes promoted here (see that file's header).

 WG0 proved handles. WG1 puts a REAL window on the screen — Mica backdrop,
 dark-mode-aware titlebar, correct DPI — created by Smalltalk, owned by a UI
 VM on the process's main thread, with Rust holding only the message pump.

 ── D1: who owns what ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   window class, creation, styles, title, DPI, the DWM calls .. HERE
   GetMessageW/TranslateMessage/DispatchMessageW ............. win_gui (Rust)
   what each message MEANS .................................. WG2, not yet

 Rust holds the pump for the three reasons D4 states — it must run before
 this file has loaded, it must survive a guest fault, and GetMessageW blocks
 where a Smalltalk `[true] whileTrue:` would starve the control channel.
 Nothing below opens the WndProc door: DefWindowProcW answers every message
 and WG2 owns that risk entirely.

 ── Three classes, one seam each ─────────────────────────────────────────
   WinArena  GC-stable foreign memory + the UTF-16 boundary. Promoted out
             of WG0's WinProbe, which now delegates to it: there is ONE
             arena per VM and one place that knows a scavenge moves things.
   WinApi    the FFI façade — every binding, every winkb constant, every
             struct offset. No policy: it answers what Win32 and the
             database say.
   WinShell  the window's Smalltalk owner. The only class here that has an
             opinion about what a window should look like.

 ── What measurement corrected in this file's own plan ───────────────────
 * D3 says `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext(PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2)`, and
   winkb carries DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2 as -4 — a
   `HANDLE` spelled as a cast negative, which marshals to the identical
   register bits the header's (DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT)-4 would. It is asked
   for by name like every other constant; nothing here transcribes -4.
 * `AdjustWindowRectEx` is NOT how this file sizes the window. It is
   DPI-ignorant (its DPI-aware twin needs the DPI you are trying to
   discover) and it models a frame DWM may not actually draw. `applyDips`
   MEASURES the frame instead — create, read the window rect and the client
   rect, and grow the window by exactly the difference — which is
   DPI-correct by construction and makes the gate's `client = DIP * dpi/96`
   an equality rather than an approximation.
 * HKEY_CURRENT_USER arrives from the database as -2147483647, i.e. C's own
   `(LONG)0x80000001`, and marshals sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFF80000001 —
   which IS the 64-bit predefined key value. The signedness rule WG0 found
   for CW_USEDEFAULT holds for the registry roots too."

"── WinArena ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 One VirtualAlloc'd region per VM, bump-allocated, never reclaimed.

 WG0's Δ 5 is the whole reason I exist: a direct `Alien new:` is ordinary
 GC-managed storage that a scavenge RELOCATES, so its address may never
 cross the FFI (world/61a's NativeBuffer rule) — and `Alien` publishes no
 address accessor to the guest at all, so a conversion answering an Alien
 can never serve Win32 by itself. Every byte Win32 reads — a WNDCLASSW, a
 class name, a window title, a RECT it writes back into — is here.

 Two allocation disciplines, because WG1 found WG0's single one
 insufficient:
   * `alloc:` — permanent. Class names, structs that outlive a call.
   * `scratchUtf16:` — a fixed, REUSED block for transient strings. A
     window title changes; memoising every title into a bump allocator
     that never reclaims is a slow leak with a cliff at the end of the
     region, which is exactly what WG0's own comment predicted WG1 would
     have to answer."
Object subclass: WinArena [
    <classVars: Base Next Limit Utf16Cache ScratchBase ScratchBytes>

    WinArena class >> bytes ^ <Integer> [
        "256 KiB. WG0 used 64; the point is still stability, not capacity,
         but a shell with a class name, a WNDCLASSW, two DWM cells, a RECT,
         a registry cell and a title buffer wants headroom that a probe did
         not."
        ^262144
    ]

    WinArena class >> scratchBytes ^ <Integer> [ ^8192 ]

    WinArena class >> base ^ <Integer> [
        "The region's address, allocating it on first use. Answers the same
         number for the life of the VM, which is also what makes it a sound
         per-VM discriminator for window-class names (WG0's Δ 11)."
        | addr |
        Base isNil ifTrue: [
            addr := WinApi
                virtualAlloc: 0
                size: self bytes
                type: ((WinApi constant: 'MEM_COMMIT') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'MEM_RESERVE'))
                protect: (WinApi constant: 'PAGE_READWRITE').
            (addr isNil or: [ addr = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
                ^self error: 'WinArena: VirtualAlloc of the ' , self bytes printString ,
                    '-byte arena failed (GetLastError ' , WinApi lastError printString , ')' ].
            Base := addr.
            Next := 0.
            Limit := self bytes ].
        ^Base
    ]

    WinArena class >> alloc: nBytes <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "The ADDRESS of nBytes of zeroed, 8-aligned, GC-stable storage.
         VirtualAlloc zero-fills its pages and nothing here ever re-uses a
         block, so the zeroing holds — which is what lets a struct's unset
         members (hIcon, lpszMenuName, a RECT before Win32 writes it) be
         reliably NULL without a memset this dialect does not have."
        | base off end |
        base := self base.
        off := Next.
        end := off + nBytes.
        end > Limit ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinArena: exhausted (' , Limit printString , ' bytes, ' ,
                off printString , ' used, ' , nBytes printString , ' wanted)' ].
        Next := end + ((8 - (end \\ 8)) \\ 8).
        ^base + off
    ]

    WinArena class >> used ^ <Integer> [ ^Next isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ Next ] ]

    WinArena class >> release [
        "Give the region back and forget every address derived from it. Any
         Alien still wrapping one of those addresses is now dangling, and any
         HWND whose class name lived here is now registered against a string
         that no longer exists — this is a teardown verb for a test that
         wants a clean slate, not a general-purpose free."
        Base isNil ifFalse: [
            WinApi virtualFree: Base size: 0 type: (WinApi constant: 'MEM_RELEASE').
            Base := nil. Next := nil. Limit := nil.
            Utf16Cache := nil. ScratchBase := nil. ScratchBytes := nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── the UTF-16 boundary ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinArena class >> nativeUtf16: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "A permanent, GC-stable copy of aString as NUL-terminated UTF-16LE;
         answers its ADDRESS. Memoised by string value — not as an
         optimisation but because the caller is usually a class name that
         Win32 keeps a pointer to forever, and re-encoding it on every
         registration would consume the arena a few hundred bytes at a time.
         The cache is safe only because these strings are literals; a caller
         that MUTATED one it had already passed here would get the old
         bytes, which is why nothing does."
        | src n addr dst hit |
        Utf16Cache isNil ifTrue: [ Utf16Cache := Dictionary new ].
        hit := Utf16Cache at: aString ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        hit isNil ifFalse: [ ^hit ].
        src := aString asUtf16Alien.
        n := src size.
        addr := self alloc: n.
        dst := Alien forAddress: addr size: n.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | dst byteAt: i put: (src byteAt: i) ].
        Utf16Cache at: aString put: addr.
        ^addr
    ]

    WinArena class >> scratchUtf16: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "aString as UTF-16LE in a REUSED block — for a string Win32 reads
         once and does not retain (a window title, a registry value name).
         Every call clobbers the previous one, which is the contract: the
         address is valid until the next call, and callers that need
         otherwise ask for `nativeUtf16:`."
        | src n dst |
        ScratchBase isNil ifTrue: [
            ScratchBase := self alloc: self scratchBytes.
            ScratchBytes := self scratchBytes ].
        src := aString asUtf16Alien.
        n := src size.
        n > ScratchBytes ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinArena: ' , n printString , ' bytes of UTF-16 will not fit the ' ,
                ScratchBytes printString , '-byte scratch block' ].
        dst := Alien forAddress: ScratchBase size: ScratchBytes.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | dst byteAt: i put: (src byteAt: i) ].
        ^ScratchBase
    ]

    WinArena class >> nativeAscii: aString <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "The narrow twin, needed where Win32 still takes a PSTR —
         GetProcAddress's symbol name, which is ASCII by definition."
        | n addr dst |
        n := aString size + 1.
        addr := self alloc: n.
        dst := Alien forAddress: addr size: n.
        1 to: aString size do: [ :i | dst byteAt: i put: (aString at: i) asInteger ].
        dst byteAt: n put: 0.
        ^addr
    ]

    WinArena class >> stringFromUtf16At: addr <Integer> units: n <Integer> ^ <String> [
        "The inverse of `String>>asUtf16Alien`, reading n UTF-16LE code units
         out of foreign memory and answering a UTF-8 String — which is what
         a String is in this dialect (world/83 decodes one as UTF-8, and
         pretending otherwise is what a text layer is for).

         Surrogate pairs are recombined; a LONE surrogate is passed through
         as its own code point rather than raising, because this decodes what
         Win32 actually handed back and Win32's UTF-16 is not guaranteed
         well-formed. GetWindowTextW's own output is, but a general reader
         that raises on the first oddity is a reader nobody can use."
        | out i hi lo cp |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        i := 0.
        [ i < n ] whileTrue: [
            hi := self u16At: addr + (i * 2).
            cp := hi.
            (hi >= 16rD800 and: [ hi <= 16rDBFF and: [ i + 1 < n ] ]) ifTrue: [
                lo := self u16At: addr + ((i + 1) * 2).
                (lo >= 16rDC00 and: [ lo <= 16rDFFF ]) ifTrue: [
                    cp := 16r10000 + ((hi - 16rD800) bitShift: 10) + (lo - 16rDC00).
                    i := i + 1 ] ].
            self utf8Of: cp into: out.
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^self stringFromBytes: out
    ]

    WinArena class >> utf8Of: cp <Integer> into: aCollection <OrderedCollection> [
        "One Unicode scalar as UTF-8 bytes, appended. The encoder world/83
         never needed because nothing until now read text back OUT of a
         foreign buffer."
        cp <= 16r7F ifTrue: [ ^aCollection add: cp ].
        cp <= 16r7FF ifTrue: [
            aCollection add: 16rC0 + (cp bitShift: -6).
            ^aCollection add: 16r80 + (cp bitAnd: 16r3F) ].
        cp <= 16rFFFF ifTrue: [
            aCollection add: 16rE0 + (cp bitShift: -12).
            aCollection add: 16r80 + ((cp bitShift: -6) bitAnd: 16r3F).
            ^aCollection add: 16r80 + (cp bitAnd: 16r3F) ].
        aCollection add: 16rF0 + (cp bitShift: -18).
        aCollection add: 16r80 + ((cp bitShift: -12) bitAnd: 16r3F).
        aCollection add: 16r80 + ((cp bitShift: -6) bitAnd: 16r3F).
        ^aCollection add: 16r80 + (cp bitAnd: 16r3F)
    ]

    WinArena class >> stringFromBytes: aCollection <OrderedCollection> ^ <String> [
        | s i |
        s := String new: aCollection size.
        i := 1.
        aCollection do: [ :b | s at: i put: b asCharacter. i := i + 1 ].
        ^s
    ]

    "── reading and writing foreign memory ───────────────────────────────
     `Alien`'s accessor family is 1-byte, 8-byte and double (S20's
     primitives), so the 4-byte members every Win32 struct is full of —
     RECT's four LONGs, a DWORD the registry writes — are assembled a byte
     at a time. Explicit little-endian, on purpose: this is exactly the kind
     of place where an implicit assumption would produce a plausible wrong
     number instead of an error."

    WinArena class >> u8At: addr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        ^(Alien forAddress: addr size: 1) byteAt: 1
    ]

    WinArena class >> u16At: addr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | a |
        a := Alien forAddress: addr size: 2.
        ^(a byteAt: 1) + ((a byteAt: 2) bitShift: 8)
    ]

    WinArena class >> u32At: addr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | a |
        a := Alien forAddress: addr size: 4.
        ^(a byteAt: 1)
            + ((a byteAt: 2) bitShift: 8)
            + ((a byteAt: 3) bitShift: 16)
            + ((a byteAt: 4) bitShift: 24)
    ]

    WinArena class >> i32At: addr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        ^WinApi i32: (self u32At: addr)
    ]

    WinArena class >> u32At: addr <Integer> put: v <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | a n |
        a := Alien forAddress: addr size: 4.
        n := v < 0 ifTrue: [ v + 4294967296 ] ifFalse: [ v ].
        a byteAt: 1 put: (n bitAnd: 255).
        a byteAt: 2 put: ((n bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255).
        a byteAt: 3 put: ((n bitShift: -16) bitAnd: 255).
        a byteAt: 4 put: ((n bitShift: -24) bitAnd: 255).
        ^v
    ]

    WinArena class >> i64At: addr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        ^(Alien forAddress: addr size: 8) signedLongAt: 1
    ]

    WinArena class >> i64At: addr <Integer> put: v <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        ^(Alien forAddress: addr size: 8) signedLongAt: 1 put: v
    ]

    WinArena class >> rectAt: addr <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "A RECT as #(left top right bottom), each member read at the offset
         winkb records — never at a transcribed one, same rule as every
         other struct in this layer."
        ^Array
            with: (self i32At: addr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'left'))
            with: (self i32At: addr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'top'))
            with: (self i32At: addr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'right'))
            with: (self i32At: addr + (WinApi offsetOf: 'RECT' field: 'bottom'))
    ]
]

"── WinApi ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 The FFI façade: every binding this layer calls, every constant it names,
 every struct offset it writes at. I hold no state a window would recognise
 and make no decision — I answer what winkb and Win32 say. That separation
 is what lets WinShell be readable as a UI class rather than as a pile of
 pragmas.

 Every parameter and return below is g-class: Win32's handles, BOOLs, ATOMs
 and HRESULTs are all FP-free composites of 8 bytes or fewer, which the
 MS-ARM64 classifier models as one GPR. The arity, float mask and return
 class must match what windows_api.db records or the P5 resolver refuses the
 call loudly BEFORE it happens.

 GetLastError is bound here and is a DIAGNOSTIC ONLY (WG0's Δ 10): the VM's
 own tier-1 compilation, W^X VirtualProtect flips and code-cache
 VirtualAlloc all run between two guest sends and all reset the thread's
 last error, so no guest-side sequencing can make it a control input. Ask
 Win32 the real question instead — GetClassInfoW, IsWindow, the return
 value, the HRESULT."
Object subclass: WinApi [
    <classVars: ConstantCache OffsetCache SizeCache User32Handle DefWindowProcAddr
                ClassInfoScratch RegDataScratch RegSizeScratch>

    "── the winkb data channel (WG0's primitives 268-271) ────────────────"

    WinApi class >> primWinkbAvailable ^ <Boolean> [
        <primitive: 268>
        ^false
    ]
    WinApi class >> primWinkbConstant: aName <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 269>
        ^nil
    ]
    WinApi class >> primWinkbStructField: aStruct <String> field: aField <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 270>
        ^nil
    ]
    WinApi class >> primWinkbStructSize: aStruct <String> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 271>
        ^nil
    ]

    WinApi class >> winkbAvailable ^ <Boolean> [
        "Is windows_api.db on this machine? A ~90 MB machine-local artifact,
         never a build input — absence is not an error, it is a reason to
         skip out loud. Answers false on macOS too, which is what lets this
         file LOAD everywhere and only RUN here."
        ^self primWinkbAvailable == true
    ]

    WinApi class >> constant: aName <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "A named Win32 constant, from the database (D2). NEVER transcribe one
         of these: a hand-copied number does not fault, it produces a window
         with the wrong style and no error anywhere. Values arrive with C's
         own signedness — CW_USEDEFAULT is -2147483648, HKEY_CURRENT_USER is
         -2147483647, DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2 is -4 —
         which is both what the header says and the only form that fits a
         SmallInt and can therefore be marshalled at all.

         Cached, because a shell asks for the same twenty flags on every
         window it opens and each miss is a SQLite query. Only HITS are
         cached: an unknown name must raise every time, not once."
        | v |
        ConstantCache isNil ifTrue: [ ConstantCache := Dictionary new ].
        v := ConstantCache at: aName ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        v isNil ifFalse: [ ^v ].
        v := self primWinkbConstant: aName.
        v isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'winkb: no constant named ' , aName printString ,
                (self winkbAvailable
                    ifTrue: [ ' in windows_api.db' ]
                    ifFalse: [ ' — and windows_api.db is not on this machine' ]) ].
        ConstantCache at: aName put: v.
        ^v
    ]

    WinApi class >> offsetOf: aStruct <String> field: aField <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "A member's byte offset, 0-based as C counts it — what makes a
         Smalltalk-built struct correct by DERIVATION rather than by
         transcription: the guest asks where lpfnWndProc lives and writes
         there, so a padding rule this file never learned still comes out
         right."
        | key v |
        OffsetCache isNil ifTrue: [ OffsetCache := Dictionary new ].
        key := aStruct , '.' , aField.
        v := OffsetCache at: key ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        v isNil ifFalse: [ ^v ].
        v := self primWinkbStructField: aStruct field: aField.
        v isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'winkb: no field ' , key , ' recorded' ].
        OffsetCache at: key put: v.
        ^v
    ]

    WinApi class >> sizeOf: aStruct <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "The struct's total size — the allocation half of build-by-layout.
         Deriving it from the last member's offset would silently drop TAIL
         PADDING, and it comes from a different database column than the
         offsets do, which is what makes comparing the two a real cross-check
         rather than a tautology."
        | v |
        SizeCache isNil ifTrue: [ SizeCache := Dictionary new ].
        v := SizeCache at: aStruct ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        v isNil ifFalse: [ ^v ].
        v := self primWinkbStructSize: aStruct.
        v isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'winkb: no struct named ' , aStruct , ' recorded' ].
        SizeCache at: aStruct put: v.
        ^v
    ]

    "── narrowing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> i32: v <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Narrow a C `int` read raw out of x0. AAPCS64 leaves the upper half
         UNSPECIFIED for a 32-bit return and Windows materialises it with
         w-register writes, i.e. zero-extended: an i32 -1 arrives as
         4294967295 through `ret: #g`. The width is not expressible in the
         g/f token vocabulary, so the binding's author owns it (P5's Δ 1)."
        ^v >= 2147483648 ifTrue: [ v - 4294967296 ] ifFalse: [ v ]
    ]

    WinApi class >> asBool: v <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Win32 BOOL: any nonzero is true. Never compare against 1 — several
         user32 entries answer other nonzero values on success."
        ^(self i32: v) ~= 0
    ]

    WinApi class >> hresultOk: v <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "An HRESULT is a SIGNED 32-bit code and failure is exactly 'negative'
         — S_OK is 0, but so are several other successes, and E_INVALIDARG
         (0x80070057) arrives zero-extended as 2147942487 through `ret: #g`.
         Testing `= 0` would call every one of those a failure and testing
         `< 2147483648` would call them all a success."
        ^(self i32: v) >= 0
    ]

    "── memory ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> virtualAlloc: a <Integer> size: s <Integer> type: t <Integer> protect: p <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #VirtualAlloc ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> virtualFree: a <Integer> size: s <Integer> type: t <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #VirtualFree ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
    ]

    "── modules, addresses, diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> moduleHandleAt: namePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetModuleHandleW ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> procAddressIn: hModule <Integer> named: namePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetProcAddress ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> lastError ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetLastError ret: #g args: #()>
    ]
    WinApi class >> currentThreadId ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetCurrentThreadId ret: #g args: #()>
    ]

    WinApi class >> moduleHandle ^ <Integer> [
        "GetModuleHandleW(NULL) — this process's own HINSTANCE, the one every
         window class registers against. NULL is a genuine null POINTER
         argument (0), not a missing one."
        ^self moduleHandleAt: 0
    ]

    WinApi class >> user32Handle ^ <Integer> [
        | h |
        User32Handle isNil ifTrue: [
            h := self moduleHandleAt: (WinArena nativeUtf16: 'user32.dll').
            h = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'WinApi: user32.dll is not loaded in this process' ].
            User32Handle := h ].
        ^User32Handle
    ]

    WinApi class >> defWindowProcAddress ^ <Integer> [
        "DefWindowProcW's own address, for the lpfnWndProc member — the
         SYSTEM's default handler. WG1 hands Win32 this and nothing else:
         there is no door, no dispatch into Smalltalk, no Rust trampoline.
         WG2 owns that, and it will need a channel this port does not have —
         the FFI resolves by NAME and never publishes a Rust function's
         address to the guest (WG0's Δ 8)."
        | a |
        DefWindowProcAddr isNil ifTrue: [
            a := self procAddressIn: self user32Handle
                     named: (WinArena nativeAscii: 'DefWindowProcW').
            (a isNil or: [ a = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
                ^self error: 'WinApi: GetProcAddress(user32, DefWindowProcW) answered NULL' ].
            DefWindowProcAddr := a ].
        ^DefWindowProcAddr
    ]

    "── window classes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> registerClass: wndClassPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #RegisterClassW ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> unregisterClass: namePtr <Integer> instance: hInstance <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #UnregisterClassW ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> classInfo: hInstance <Integer> name: namePtr <Integer> into: wcPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetClassInfoW ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> loadCursor: hInstance <Integer> name: namePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #LoadCursorW ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> createSolidBrush: colorref <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateSolidBrush ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]

    WinApi class >> classExists: aName <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Is aName registered for this process's HINSTANCE? This is the
         question WG0 discovered GetLastError cannot answer under this VM
         (its Δ 10). GetClassInfoW fills a WNDCLASSW it is given and answers
         a BOOL; the buffer's contents are of no interest, only the answer,
         so one scratch struct serves every call."
        ClassInfoScratch isNil ifTrue: [
            ClassInfoScratch := WinArena alloc: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW') ].
        ^self asBool: (self
            classInfo: self moduleHandle
            name: (WinArena nativeUtf16: aName)
            into: ClassInfoScratch)
    ]

    WinApi class >> wndClassStyle: style <Integer> proc: procAddr <Integer> instance: hInst <Integer> cursor: hCursor <Integer> brush: hBrush <Integer> named: aName <String> ^ <Integer> [
        "Build a WNDCLASSW in GC-stable memory at winkb-QUERIED offsets and
         answer its address. Members are written with `signedLongAt:put:`
         (an 8-byte write at a 1-based byte index), which is correct for the
         two i32 members only because the arena is zeroed and both cbClsExtra
         and cbWndExtra are 0 — the write of `style` covers cbClsExtra's
         neighbouring padding with the same zeroes it already had.

         Deliberately left NULL: hIcon and lpszMenuName. A window with no
         icon takes the system default and a window with no menu is what WG1
         wants; WG3 fills them in when there is a shell to hang them on."
        | addr wc |
        addr := WinArena alloc: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW').
        wc := Alien forAddress: addr size: (self sizeOf: 'WNDCLASSW').
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'style') + 1 put: style.
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'lpfnWndProc') + 1 put: procAddr.
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'hInstance') + 1 put: hInst.
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'hCursor') + 1 put: hCursor.
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'hbrBackground') + 1 put: hBrush.
        wc signedLongAt: (self offsetOf: 'WNDCLASSW' field: 'lpszClassName') + 1
           put: (WinArena nativeUtf16: aName).
        ^addr
    ]

    "── windows ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     CreateWindowExW's TWELVE g-class arguments are the first call on either
     platform in this port to spill past x0-x7 into codecache::ffi_stubs'
     stack slots against a Win32 entry point (WG0's Δ 9); MS ARM64 and Apple
     AAPCS64 agree on 8-byte stack slots for 8-byte values, so the emitters
     written for the Mac carry it unchanged."

    WinApi class >> createWindowExStyle: exStyle <Integer> class: classPtr <Integer> title: titlePtr <Integer> style: style <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> parent: parent <Integer> menu: menu <Integer> instance: hInstance <Integer> param: lpParam <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #CreateWindowExW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g g g g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> isWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #IsWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> destroyWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #DestroyWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> showWindow: hwnd <Integer> cmd: nCmdShow <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #ShowWindow ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> updateWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #UpdateWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> setForegroundWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetForegroundWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> setWindowText: hwnd <Integer> text: textPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetWindowTextW ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> getWindowText: hwnd <Integer> buffer: bufPtr <Integer> max: cch <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetWindowTextW ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> getClientRect: hwnd <Integer> into: rectPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetClientRect ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> getWindowRect: hwnd <Integer> into: rectPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetWindowRect ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> setWindowPos: hwnd <Integer> after: hAfter <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> flags: f <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetWindowPos ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> postMessage: hwnd <Integer> message: m <Integer> wParam: w <Integer> lParam: l <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #PostMessageW ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> windowThread: hwnd <Integer> processId: pidPtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetWindowThreadProcessId ret: #g args: #(g g)>
    ]

    "── DPI (D3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> setProcessDpiAwarenessContext: ctx <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]
    WinApi class >> dpiForWindow: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetDpiForWindow ret: #g args: #(g)>
    ]

    "── Windows-11 materials (D3) ────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinApi class >> dwmSetWindowAttribute: hwnd <Integer> attribute: attr <Integer> data: dataPtr <Integer> size: n <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "dwmapi.dll, and the resolver reaches it exactly as it reaches
         user32 — winkb records the DLL and P5's fallback loads it. Answers
         an HRESULT: a build that predates an attribute answers a FAILURE
         CODE rather than faulting, which is why D3 says check the HRESULT
         and never the build number."
        <primitive: FFI function: #DwmSetWindowAttribute ret: #g args: #(g g g g)>
    ]

    "── the registry, for the system's own dark-mode preference ──────────"

    WinApi class >> regGetValue: hkey <Integer> subKey: subPtr <Integer> value: valuePtr <Integer> flags: f <Integer> type: typePtr <Integer> data: dataPtr <Integer> size: sizePtr <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #RegGetValueW ret: #g args: #(g g g g g g g)>
    ]

    WinApi class >> dwordAt: aKeyRoot <Integer> path: aPath <String> value: aValue <String> ^ <Object> [
        "One REG_DWORD, or nil if it is not there. Answers nil rather than
         raising because a missing preference is a normal state of the world
         and the caller's job is to have an honest default — the registry is
         being ASKED, not depended on."
        "The two out-parameter cells are allocated ONCE and reused. The arena
         never reclaims, so a fresh pair per call would be a slow leak in the
         one method a UI is most likely to poll — and RegGetValueW writes
         both every time, so there is nothing stale to inherit."
        | sub name data size rc |
        RegDataScratch isNil ifTrue: [
            RegDataScratch := WinArena alloc: 8.
            RegSizeScratch := WinArena alloc: 8 ].
        sub := WinArena nativeUtf16: aPath.
        name := WinArena nativeUtf16: aValue.
        data := RegDataScratch.
        size := RegSizeScratch.
        WinArena u32At: data put: 0.
        WinArena u32At: size put: 4.
        rc := self i32: (self
            regGetValue: aKeyRoot
            subKey: sub
            value: name
            flags: (self constant: 'RRF_RT_REG_DWORD')
            type: 0
            data: data
            size: size).
        rc = (self constant: 'ERROR_SUCCESS') ifFalse: [ ^nil ].
        ^WinArena u32At: data
    ]
]

"── WinShell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 I am the window. Everything a user would call 'how the environment looks'
 is decided here, in Smalltalk, in a file the Browser can open and edit
 while the window it made is on the screen.

 I do NOT own the message loop. `macvm-winui` (Rust) pumps; I create, style
 and close. That split is D1's, and D4 states its three reasons: the pump
 must run before this file has loaded, it must survive a guest fault (P2
 recovers the VM, not a loop), and GetMessageW blocks in a way a Smalltalk
 `[true] whileTrue:` could not interleave the control channel with.

 I also do not answer messages. DefWindowProcW does, unchanged, for all of
 them. WG2 opens that door and owns the whole of its risk.

 ── The order in `openMain` is load-bearing ──────────────────────────────
   1  SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext   before ANY window exists, or the
                                      process is stuck with what it had
   2  read the system's theme         before the class, because the class
                                      carries the background brush
   3  RegisterClassW                  name unique per VM (WG0's Δ 11)
   4  CreateWindowExW  (not visible)  binds the HWND to THIS thread
   5  size to DIPs by measurement     while nobody can see it resize
   6  the two DwmSetWindowAttribute   BEFORE showing, or Mica lands on an
                                      already-painted frame and flickers
   7  ShowWindow / UpdateWindow"
Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: Hwnd ClassName Brush CreatingThreadId Dpi DarkMode
                MicaHResult DarkHResult FaultMode RectScratch TextScratch DwmCell>

    "── the window's own opinions ────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> dipWidth ^ <Integer> [ ^900 ]
    WinShell class >> dipHeight ^ <Integer> [ ^600 ]
    WinShell class >> defaultTitle ^ <String> [ ^'MACVM — Windows' ]
    WinShell class >> textBufferUnits ^ <Integer> [ ^512 ]

    WinShell class >> className ^ <String> [
        "Unique per VM, because a window class is registered against the
         PROCESS and several VMs share one (WG0's Δ 11 — its suite ran two
         VMs in parallel threads and one's UnregisterClassW could pull the
         class out from under the other's CreateWindowExW). The arena's base
         address is unique per live VM and nothing ever frees it, so it is
         exactly the discriminator needed at no extra state. Stable WITHIN a
         VM, so re-registration is still genuinely exercised.

         Two `macvm-winui` processes on one desktop are a normal debugging
         situation and are covered for free — separate processes, separate
         class namespaces."
        ClassName isNil ifTrue: [
            ClassName := 'MacvmWinUiShell_' , (WinArena base printString: 16) ].
        ^ClassName
    ]

    "── theme (D3): the value comes from the system ──────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> appsUseLightTheme ^ <Object> [
        "HKCU\\...\\Themes\\Personalize\\AppsUseLightTheme — 0 dark, 1 light,
         nil if the value is not there. This is a READ, never an invention:
         the design's restraint principle says wear the host's clothes, and
         a preference this project made up would not be the host's."
        ^WinApi
            dwordAt: (WinApi constant: 'HKEY_CURRENT_USER')
            path: 'Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Themes\Personalize'
            value: 'AppsUseLightTheme'
    ]

    WinShell class >> systemPrefersDark ^ <Boolean> [
        "Light is the honest default when the read fails: a missing key means
         'this machine never said', and guessing dark would be this project
         inventing the preference D3 forbids it from inventing."
        | v |
        v := self appsUseLightTheme.
        v isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^v = 0
    ]

    WinShell class >> backgroundColorRef ^ <Integer> [
        "COLORREF is 0x00BBGGRR. Both values are neutral greys (R=G=B), so
         the channel order cannot be got wrong here by accident — which also
         means a capture of this window is a machine-checkable statement
         about which theme was read: #202020 dark, #F3F3F3 light, the Fluent
         base layers.

         Δ (WG1, nearly measured the hard way): the obvious version reads the
         LATCHED `DarkMode`, which `openMain` sets and which is nil before it.
         That answers 'light' on a machine in dark mode with no window open
         yet — an answer that is wrong, plausible, and only wrong on somebody
         else's machine, since this one runs light. So when nothing has been
         latched, ask the system; once a window exists, answer what its brush
         was actually made from, which is what a capture of it will show even
         if the user has changed themes since."
        ^(DarkMode isNil ifTrue: [ self systemPrefersDark ] ifFalse: [ DarkMode ])
            ifTrue: [ 16r202020 ] ifFalse: [ 16rF3F3F3 ]
    ]

    "── state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> hwndValue ^ <Integer> [
        "The HWND as an Integer — what the Rust host reads back through `eval`
         to point the inherited `snap` at, and to check the same-thread
         invariant against its own GetCurrentThreadId. 0 when there is no
         LIVE window.

         Δ (WG1, measured): the obvious version answers whatever `Hwnd` holds,
         and it made the first `WinShell close` in this port hang the process.
         DefWindowProcW destroyed the window, but this class still remembered
         the number, so the host asked `hwndValue`, got a plausible handle,
         concluded its window was alive and kept pumping an empty queue
         forever. A remembered handle is not a window — the same reason WinRef
         asks Win32 every time it is asked whether it is alive rather than
         caching an answer that a timestamp would make a lie."
        ^self isOpen ifTrue: [ Hwnd ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> hwnd ^ <WinRef> [
        "The handle with an honest home (WG0's WinRef). Raises rather than
         answering a null ref, because every caller of mine wants a window."
        self ensureOpen: 'hwnd'.
        ^WinRef onHandle: Hwnd
    ]

    WinShell class >> isOpen ^ <Boolean> [
        (Hwnd isNil or: [ Hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi isWindow: Hwnd)
    ]

    WinShell class >> ensureOpen: aSelector <String> [
        "A clean guest error naming the call, never a Win32 call on a null
         HWND. `WinShell setTitle:` before `openMain` is a programmer
         mistake and must read like one — SetWindowTextW(NULL, ...) would
         quietly answer TRUE and change nothing (measured), which is the
         worst of both worlds."
        self isOpen ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell ' , aSelector ,
                ': there is no window — send `WinShell openMain` first' ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> creatingThreadId ^ <Object> [ ^CreatingThreadId ]
    WinShell class >> dpi ^ <Object> [ ^Dpi ]
    WinShell class >> darkMode ^ <Object> [ ^DarkMode ]
    WinShell class >> micaHResult ^ <Object> [ ^MicaHResult ]
    WinShell class >> darkHResult ^ <Object> [ ^DarkHResult ]

    WinShell class >> windowThreadId ^ <Integer> [
        "The thread Windows says owns the window — the half of the same-
         thread invariant that Win32, not this file, is the authority on."
        self ensureOpen: 'windowThreadId'.
        ^WinApi windowThread: Hwnd processId: 0
    ]

    WinShell class >> threadInvariantHolds ^ <Boolean> [
        "True when the thread asking is the thread that owns the window.
         Asked from the pump thread, this is the assertion D2 exists for: if
         a refactor ever moves `openMain` off main, DispatchMessageW silently
         stops delivering and nothing else says so."
        ^self windowThreadId = WinApi currentThreadId
    ]

    "── scratch buffers, allocated once ──────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> rectScratch ^ <Integer> [
        RectScratch isNil ifTrue: [ RectScratch := WinArena alloc: (WinApi sizeOf: 'RECT') ].
        ^RectScratch
    ]

    WinShell class >> textScratch ^ <Integer> [
        TextScratch isNil ifTrue: [ TextScratch := WinArena alloc: self textBufferUnits * 2 ].
        ^TextScratch
    ]

    "── opening ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> ensureClassRegistered [
        "RegisterClassW, tolerating the second time. WG0's Δ 10 in one line:
         the atom is 0 both when the class already exists and when the
         registration genuinely failed, and GetLastError cannot tell them
         apart under this VM — so ask Win32 the real question with
         GetClassInfoW instead. The error message still REPORTS the code,
         and says it may be stale."
        | atom |
        atom := WinApi registerClass: (WinApi
            wndClassStyle: ((WinApi constant: 'CS_HREDRAW') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'CS_VREDRAW'))
            proc: WinApi defWindowProcAddress
            instance: WinApi moduleHandle
            cursor: (WinApi loadCursor: 0 name: (WinApi constant: 'IDC_ARROW'))
            brush: self backgroundBrush
            named: self className).
        atom ~= 0 ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        (WinApi classExists: self className) ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        ^self error: 'WinShell: RegisterClassW(' , self className ,
            ') answered 0 and the class is not registered; GetLastError = ' ,
            WinApi lastError printString , ' (which the VM may have clobbered — WG0 Δ 10)'
    ]

    WinShell class >> backgroundBrush ^ <Integer> [
        "One solid brush for the life of the VM. DefWindowProcW erases the
         client area with it on WM_ERASEBKGND, which is the whole of WG1's
         painting: the frame, the thread and the camera are what this sprint
         proves, and an empty window whose fill is a KNOWN colour is better
         evidence than an empty window whose fill is whatever was behind it."
        Brush isNil ifTrue: [ Brush := WinApi createSolidBrush: self backgroundColorRef ].
        ^Brush
    ]

    WinShell class >> openMain ^ <WinRef> [
        "The whole of D1's Smalltalk half. See this class's header for why
         the order of these steps is load-bearing."
        | hwnd |
        self isOpen ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell openMain: a window is already open (HWND 16r' ,
                (Hwnd printString: 16) , '); send `close` first' ].

        "1. DPI awareness, before any window exists (D3, and win_gui_design
            §3's third named risk: declare PerMonitorV2 at WG1, not later).
            The answer is deliberately not checked: it is a process-wide,
            once-only setting, so every call after the first answers FALSE,
            and `cycle:` makes exactly that happen ten times in a row. A
            failure here that MATTERED would show up as a client rect that
            does not scale — which `expectedClientWidth` asserts — rather
            than as a BOOL nobody could interpret."

        "2. The system's theme, before the class carries its brush."
        DarkMode := self systemPrefersDark.

        "3. The class."
        self ensureClassRegistered.

        "4. The window — NOT visible yet, so steps 5 and 6 happen where
            nobody can watch them happen."
        hwnd := WinApi
            createWindowExStyle: 0
            class: (WinArena nativeUtf16: self className)
            title: (WinArena scratchUtf16: self defaultTitle)
            style: (WinApi constant: 'WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW')
            x: (WinApi constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            y: (WinApi constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            width: (WinApi constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            height: (WinApi constant: 'CW_USEDEFAULT')
            parent: 0
            menu: 0
            instance: WinApi moduleHandle
            param: 0.
        (hwnd isNil or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell: CreateWindowExW failed (GetLastError ' ,
                WinApi lastError printString , ')' ].
        Hwnd := hwnd.
        CreatingThreadId := WinApi currentThreadId.

        "5. DPI-correct size."
        self applyDips.

        "6. The materials, BEFORE showing (D3's pitfall)."
        self applyMaterials.

        "7. On screen."
        WinApi showWindow: Hwnd cmd: (WinApi constant: 'SW_SHOW').
        WinApi updateWindow: Hwnd.
        WinApi setForegroundWindow: Hwnd.

        "A deliberately injected failure, for the gate's 'the loop outlives a
         VM fault' item (tests_wg1.md stress row 4). Armed only by the host
         when it is testing that; unarmed it costs one nil test."
        self applyFaultInjection.
        ^self hwnd
    ]

    WinShell class >> applyDips [
        "Size the CLIENT area to dipWidth x dipHeight scaled by this
         monitor's DPI, by MEASURING the frame rather than predicting it.

         Δ (WG1, measured): D3 reaches for AdjustWindowRectEx, and the plan
         is wrong twice. That call is DPI-ignorant (its DPI-aware twin wants
         the DPI you are still discovering), and it describes the frame the
         non-client metrics imply, not the one DWM actually draws — on
         Windows 11 the two differ. Creating the window first and reading
         BOTH rects gives the real delta in one step, with no constant, no
         second API and no approximation, and it makes the gate's
         `client = DIP * dpi / 96` an exact equality."
        | client window dw dh wantW wantH |
        Dpi := WinApi dpiForWindow: Hwnd.
        (Dpi isNil or: [ Dpi = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ Dpi := WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ].
        wantW := self dipWidth * Dpi // (WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI').
        wantH := self dipHeight * Dpi // (WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI').
        client := self clientRect.
        window := self windowRect.
        dw := ((window at: 3) - (window at: 1)) - ((client at: 3) - (client at: 1)).
        dh := ((window at: 4) - (window at: 2)) - ((client at: 4) - (client at: 2)).
        WinApi
            setWindowPos: Hwnd
            after: 0
            x: 0
            y: 0
            width: wantW + dw
            height: wantH + dh
            flags: ((WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOMOVE') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'SWP_NOZORDER')).
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> applyMaterials [
        "The three Windows-11 materials of §2.1, all from Smalltalk, all
         through dwmapi:
           Mica backdrop   DWMWA_SYSTEMBACKDROP_TYPE := DWMSBT_MAINWINDOW
           dark titlebar   DWMWA_USE_IMMERSIVE_DARK_MODE := the SYSTEM's value
           rounded corners nothing to call — Win11 does it for free

         Both HRESULTs are RECORDED and neither is fatal. A Windows build
         that predates an attribute answers a failure code and the window is
         simply opaque or light-framed; that is a legitimate outcome, and
         checking the HRESULT rather than the build number is the same
         discipline as asking GetClassInfoW rather than reading an error
         code (D3).

         The 4-byte value cell is allocated once and reused across both
         calls and every re-open: DwmSetWindowAttribute COPIES what it is
         given before returning, so nothing here outlives the call."
        | cell |
        DwmCell isNil ifTrue: [ DwmCell := WinArena alloc: 8 ].
        cell := DwmCell.

        WinArena u32At: cell put: (WinApi constant: 'DWMSBT_MAINWINDOW').
        MicaHResult := WinApi i32: (WinApi
            dwmSetWindowAttribute: Hwnd
            attribute: (WinApi constant: 'DWMWA_SYSTEMBACKDROP_TYPE')
            data: cell
            size: 4).

        WinArena u32At: cell put: (DarkMode == true ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]).
        DarkHResult := WinApi i32: (WinApi
            dwmSetWindowAttribute: Hwnd
            attribute: (WinApi constant: 'DWMWA_USE_IMMERSIVE_DARK_MODE')
            data: cell
            size: 4).
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> micaTook ^ <Boolean> [ ^MicaHResult notNil and: [ MicaHResult >= 0 ] ]
    WinShell class >> darkTitlebarTook ^ <Boolean> [ ^DarkHResult notNil and: [ DarkHResult >= 0 ] ]

    "── geometry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> clientRect ^ <Array> [
        self ensureOpen: 'clientRect'.
        (WinApi asBool: (WinApi getClientRect: Hwnd into: self rectScratch)) ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell: GetClientRect failed (GetLastError ' ,
                WinApi lastError printString , ')' ].
        ^WinArena rectAt: self rectScratch
    ]

    WinShell class >> windowRect ^ <Array> [
        self ensureOpen: 'windowRect'.
        (WinApi asBool: (WinApi getWindowRect: Hwnd into: self rectScratch)) ifFalse: [
            ^self error: 'WinShell: GetWindowRect failed (GetLastError ' ,
                WinApi lastError printString , ')' ].
        ^WinArena rectAt: self rectScratch
    ]

    WinShell class >> clientWidth ^ <Integer> [
        | r | r := self clientRect. ^(r at: 3) - (r at: 1)
    ]
    WinShell class >> clientHeight ^ <Integer> [
        | r | r := self clientRect. ^(r at: 4) - (r at: 2)
    ]

    WinShell class >> expectedClientWidth ^ <Integer> [
        "What the DIP width SHOULD be at this window's DPI — the gate's
         arithmetic, written once here so the test and the report cannot
         drift from each other."
        ^self dipWidth * self dpi // (WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI')
    ]
    WinShell class >> expectedClientHeight ^ <Integer> [
        ^self dipHeight * self dpi // (WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI')
    ]

    "── title ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> setTitle: aString <String> ^ <Boolean> [
        self ensureOpen: 'setTitle:'.
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi setWindowText: Hwnd
                                text: (WinArena scratchUtf16: aString))
    ]

    WinShell class >> title ^ <String> [
        "Read back from the WINDOW, not from a variable this class kept —
         the gate's item 5 is 'the titlebar really changed', and a cached
         copy could not tell the difference between that and a no-op."
        | n |
        self ensureOpen: 'title'.
        n := WinApi i32: (WinApi getWindowText: Hwnd
                                buffer: self textScratch
                                max: self textBufferUnits).
        n < 0 ifTrue: [ n := 0 ].
        ^WinArena stringFromUtf16At: self textScratch units: n
    ]

    "── closing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> close ^ <Boolean> [
        "POST WM_CLOSE — the same message the titlebar's X sends, so the
         script path and the human path are one path. DefWindowProcW turns it
         into DestroyWindow (D1: WG1 answers no message itself).

         Closing is TWO events, not one: WM_CLOSE destroys the window,
         WM_DESTROY should PostQuitMessage so the pump's GetMessageW returns
         0. The second half needs the door WG2 builds, so for WG1 the host's
         pump notices its window has gone and posts the quit itself — see
         win_gui's `pump`, which records the same thing from the other side."
        self ensureOpen: 'close'.
        ^WinApi asBool: (WinApi postMessage: Hwnd
                                message: (WinApi constant: 'WM_CLOSE')
                                wParam: 0
                                lParam: 0)
    ]

    WinShell class >> destroyNow ^ <Boolean> [
        "Synchronous teardown, for a test that opens and closes many windows
         in one process without a pump to deliver WM_CLOSE. DestroyWindow
         must be called on the owning thread, which is where a doit runs."
        | ok |
        self isOpen ifFalse: [ Hwnd := nil. ^false ].
        ok := WinApi asBool: (WinApi destroyWindow: Hwnd).
        Hwnd := nil.
        ^ok
    ]

    WinShell class >> forget [
        "Drop the window reference without touching Win32 — for a host that
         has just watched its window disappear and wants this class to agree."
        Hwnd := nil.
        ^self
    ]

    "── fault injection, for the gate's 'the loop outlives a fault' item ─"

    WinShell class >> faultMode: aSymbol <Symbol> [
        "#guest raises an ordinary Smalltalk error; #native dereferences
         address 1, which is a genuine access violation recovered by P2's
         VEH + non-unwinding longjmp. Both are injected at the END of
         `openMain`, so the window is fully real and fully shown when the
         VM falls over — which is the arrangement the gate needs: the pump
         must outlive the fault with a live window still in front of it."
        FaultMode := aSymbol.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> applyFaultInjection [
        FaultMode isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        FaultMode == #guest ifTrue: [
            FaultMode := nil.
            ^self error: 'WinShell: deliberate guest fault inside openMain (MACVM_WINUI_FAULT)' ].
        FaultMode == #native ifTrue: [
            FaultMode := nil.
            ^(Alien forAddress: 1 size: 8) signedLongAt: 1 ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── reporting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> report [
        "Every fact the sprint report has to state, in one doit. Written for
         a human reading a terminal and for a grep in a gate script; both
         audiences are real."
        Transcript showCr: 'WinShell:'.
        Transcript showCr: '  class          ' , self className.
        Transcript showCr: '  hwnd           16r' , (self hwndValue printString: 16).
        Transcript showCr: '  isWindow       ' , self isOpen printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  title          ' , (self isOpen ifTrue: [ self title ] ifFalse: [ '<none>' ]).
        Transcript showCr: '  dpi            ' , self dpi printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  client         ' , (self isOpen
            ifTrue: [ self clientWidth printString , 'x' , self clientHeight printString ]
            ifFalse: [ '<none>' ]).
        Transcript showCr: '  expected       ' , (self isOpen
            ifTrue: [ self expectedClientWidth printString , 'x' , self expectedClientHeight printString ]
            ifFalse: [ '<none>' ]).
        Transcript showCr: '  dark preferred ' , self darkMode printString ,
            ' (AppsUseLightTheme = ' , self appsUseLightTheme printString , ')'.
        Transcript showCr: '  mica HRESULT   ' , self micaHResult printString ,
            ' (' , self micaTook printString , ')'.
        Transcript showCr: '  dark HRESULT   ' , self darkHResult printString ,
            ' (' , self darkTitlebarTook printString , ')'.
        Transcript showCr: '  created on tid ' , self creatingThreadId printString.
        Transcript showCr: '  owned by tid   ' , (self isOpen
            ifTrue: [ self windowThreadId printString ] ifFalse: [ '<none>' ]).
        Transcript showCr: '  this tid       ' , WinApi currentThreadId printString.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> cycle: n <Integer> ^ <Dictionary> [
        "Open and destroy the window n times in one process (tests_wg1.md
         stress row 2). What this is really testing is that the class
         registration does not leak and that no HWND is handed back twice:
         RegisterClassW answers 0 from the second call on, and a shell that
         only ever opened once would pass in isolation and fail forever
         after — the exact trap WG0's own re-run pitfall laid for WG1.

         The window really is created and shown each time, because a hidden
         window would not exercise what WG1 changed."
        | handles usable |
        handles := Set new.
        usable := true.
        1 to: n do: [ :i |
            self openMain.
            handles add: self hwndValue.
            (WinApi classExists: self className) ifFalse: [ usable := false ].
            self destroyNow ].
        ^(Dictionary new)
            at: 'opened' put: n;
            at: 'distinctHandles' put: handles size;
            at: 'classUsableEveryTime' put: usable;
            at: 'stillRegistered' put: (WinApi classExists: self className);
            yourself
    ]
]
